{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Using this dataset's total farmers markets, and the census file: how many farmers markets does the U.S. have per 100,000 residents, to four decimal places? One confident sentence. Do not mention unverified facts and use tools to do any math necessary. Data sources: https://harvestlymarkets.com/data/us-farmers-markets-by-state-2026.csv and https://gist.githubusercontent.com/hussainsultan/369600f5179b22792a2b4c9ed37de056/raw/NST-EST2025-ALLDATA.csv"}],"timestamp":1786914665724},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking for a calculation based on two data sources:\n1. A dataset of U.S. farmers markets by state (https://harvestlymarkets.com/data/us-farmers-markets-by-state-2026.csv)\n2. A census file with population data (https://gist.githubusercontent.com/hussainsultan/369600f5179b22792a2b4c9ed37de056/raw/NST-EST2025-ALLDATA.csv)\n\nThe question is: How many farmers markets does the U.S. have per 100,000 residents?\n\nFollowing the xorq analyst protocol, I need to:\n1. First check if there are semantic models in the catalog\n2. Check what aliases exist\n3. Ingest these data sources into the catalog if they're not already there\n4. Compose a metric that calculates: (total farmers markets / total residents) * 100,000\n5. Use xorq_select to get the value\n6. Use xorq_verify to verify the answer\n7. Verify the source lineage\n\nLet me start by orienting myself with the catalog.","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll help you find this verified answer. Let me start by checking the xorq catalog for existing data and semantic models."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_016ZqshMYsMA8gpGFLu4sgE6","name":"xorq_semantic_models","arguments":{"catalog_path":".xorq/catalog"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01N21dufn6dFnUanzPzVxbVN","name":"xorq_catalog_list_aliases","arguments":{"catalog_path":".xorq/catalog"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"ant